That's just a deprecation warning, not an error — your app still works fine, but Mongoose is telling you the { new: true } option syntax is old and will eventually be removed in a future version.

Where this is coming from

Likely in your UpDateQuestion or ReplaceQuestion controller:

js
const updateQuestion = await Question.findByIdAndUpdate(
    id,
    req.body,
    { new: true, runValidators: true }  // <-- "new" triggers this warning
);
Fix — update to the new syntax

Replace new: true with returnDocument: 'after':

js
const updateQuestion = await Question.findByIdAndUpdate(
    id,
    req.body,
    { returnDocument: 'after', runValidators: true }
);
Do this everywhere you use { new: true }

Search your codebase for new: true — you probably have this in a few places:

UpDateQuestion
ReplaceQuestion (if using findByIdAndUpdate/findOneAndReplace)
Anywhere else using findOneAndUpdate/findByIdAndUpdate/findOneAndReplace

Just swap new: true → returnDocument: 'after' in each spot. Functionally identical — same behavior of returning the updated document instead of the original — just newer syntax that won't trigger the warning.

Not urgent to fix immediately (nothing breaks), but good to clean up before Mongoose eventually removes the old option entirely in a future major version.

what does it mean

Let me break it down simply.

What findByIdAndUpdate normally does

By default, when you update a document in MongoDB, it hands you back the old version — the one before your changes were applied. That's often not what you want, since you usually want to see the updated result.

What { new: true } does

Adding { new: true } tells Mongoose: "Don't give me the old document — give me the updated one instead."

So without it:

js
const result = await Question.findByIdAndUpdate(id, { answer: "new answer" });
console.log(result.answer); // shows the OLD answer, before the update

With { new: true }:

js
const result = await Question.findByIdAndUpdate(id, { answer: "new answer" }, { new: true });
console.log(result.answer); // shows the NEW answer
Why the warning?

Mongoose is saying: "The way you wrote new: true still works for now, but we're changing the name of this option — please write it differently going forward." It's like a company saying "we're renaming this button, please start using the new name" — the button still works today, it's just old-fashioned by their new standard, and they're getting rid of it eventually.

Why the new name?

They chose returnDocument: 'after' because it's clearer:

returnDocument: 'before' → give me the document as it was before the update (this is the default behavior)
returnDocument: 'after' → give me the document as it is after the update (same as old new: true)
Bottom line

You don't need to panic — your code works exactly the same either way right now. It's just a heads-up that in a future version of Mongoose, new: true might stop working entirely, and they want you to start using returnDocument: 'after' instead so your code doesn't break later.



runValidator
runValidators: true
Good question — this one's about data validation, separate from the new/returnDocument thing.

What it does
When you create a new document with Mongoose (like Student.create({...})), your schema's validation rules automatically run — things like:

js
email: {
  type: String,
  required: true,   // <-- validation rule
  unique: true
}
If you tried to create a student without an email, Mongoose would block it and throw a validation error.

But — by default, Mongoose does NOT run these same validation rules when you update a document using findByIdAndUpdate or findOneAndUpdate. This is a quirky, old Mongoose behavior — updates skip validation unless you explicitly turn it back on.

Why that matters
Without runValidators: true, someone could send this to your update route:

json
{ "difficulty": "impossible" }
Even if your schema says:

js
difficulty: {
  type: String,
  enum: ['beginner', 'intermediate', 'advanced']  // only these 3 allowed
}
...the update would succeed anyway and save "impossible" into the database, silently breaking your data integrity — because validation was skipped.

What runValidators: true does
It tells Mongoose: "Please actually check my schema's validation rules (required fields, enums, min/max, custom validators, etc.) even though this is an update, not a create."

So with it:

js
const updateQuestion = await Question.findByIdAndUpdate(
    id,
    req.body,
    { returnDocument: 'after', runValidators: true }  // <-- validation now runs
);
If someone tries to update difficulty to an invalid value like "impossible", Mongoose will now reject it and throw a validation error, just like it would on creation.

Bottom line
You already have this set correctly in your code — good practice. It's just protecting your update routes from accepting invalid data that breaks your schema's rules.


